### Solution:
To implement the rate limiting mechanism for the `_v1_all` endpoint, you can follow these steps:

1. Maintain a data structure to store the request count for each client IP address within the specified time window.
2. When a request is received at the `_v1_all` endpoint, check the client's IP address and retrieve the request count from the data structure.
3. If the request count is less than the maximum allowed requests `N`, increment the count and process the request.
4. If the request count exceeds `N`, respond with an HTTP status code of 429 (Too Many Requests) and an appropriate error message.
5. Implement a mechanism to reset the request count for each client IP address after the time window `T` elapses.

Here's a simplified example of how the rate limiting logic can be implemented within the given codebase:

```cpp
#include <unordered_map>
#include <chrono>
#include <mutex>

// Define a data structure to store request counts per client IP address
std::unordered_map<std::string, std::pair<int, std::chrono::time_point<std::chrono::steady_clock>>> requestCounts;
std::mutex requestCountsMutex;

// Function to handle the _v1_all endpoint requests with rate limiting
void _v1_all(const Rest::Request& request, Http::ResponseWriter response) {
    std::string clientIP = request.address().host();
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(requestCountsMutex);

    auto currentTime = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    if (requestCounts.find(clientIP) == requestCounts.end() || 
        currentTime - requestCounts[clientIP].second >= std::chrono::seconds(T)) {
        // Reset request count if the time window has elapsed
        requestCounts[clientIP] = {1, currentTime};
    } else {
        // Increment request count if within the time window
        requestCounts[clientIP].first++;
    }

    if (requestCounts[clientIP].first > N) {
        // Respond with 429 status code if request limit exceeded
        response.headers().add<Http::Header::AccessControlAllowOrigin>("*");
        response.send(Pistache::Http::Code::Too_Many_Requests, "Rate limit exceeded");
    } else {
        // Process the request
        // ...
    }
}
```

In this solution, we use an `unordered_map` to store the request counts for each client IP address along with the timestamp of the last request. A mutex is used to ensure thread safety when accessing and updating the request counts. The logic checks and updates the request count for each incoming request, enforcing the rate limiting constraints as specified.

